## 2. Remaining finding R1 — action sets and policy classes are still conflated

### Severity

**Critical formal correction.**

### Current problem

The architecture defines an implementable action hierarchy

\[
U_{\mathrm{impl}}\subseteq U_{\mathrm{inst}}\subseteq U_{\mathrm{tech}}\subseteq U_{\mathrm{theor}},
\]

but Operator I writes, in effect,

\[
\exists\pi\in U_q^{\mathrm{impl}}.
\]

A causal policy \(\pi\) is not an instantaneous action. It belongs to a policy class whose outputs lie in an action correspondence. The distinction becomes essential under partial observation, delayed review, decentralized actors, and strategic institutions.

### Required incorporation

Introduce:

- action correspondence \(U_q^{\mathrm{impl}}(z)\);
- causal information pattern \(\mathcal I_q\);
- implementable policy class \(\mathbb P_q^{\mathrm{impl}}\);
- disturbance strategy class \(\Delta_q\) or disturbance signal class with nonanticipation rules.

Then write

\[
\operatorname{RViab}_{\mathcal A_q}
(\mathcal V_\Omega)
=
\left\{
z_0:
\exists\pi\in\mathbb P_q^{\mathrm{impl}}
\ \forall\delta\in\Delta_q,
\ z^{\pi,\delta}(t)
\in\mathcal V_\Omega
\right\}.
\]

Article 001 supplies controlled, robust, epistemic, and institutional policy hierarchies. Article 002 supplies causal observation-based policy classes and restricted constructive kernels.

